<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
<Document>
  <name>Saltas Family August "SPRING" Break — Murray to Lava Hot Springs Road Trip</name>
  <description><![CDATA[Utah · Idaho — Sunday Drive. North on I-15 through Malad and McCammon to the hot pools, then home by Soda Springs, Preston, and Logan — Sunday, August 16. Gold-marked stops are soaking springs. 15 stops · 161.7 mi outbound · 202.7 mi return · ~7h 30m total drive time.]]></description>
  <Style id="startPin"><IconStyle><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/grn-stars.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="springPin"><IconStyle><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/blu-circle.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="midPin"><IconStyle><Icon><href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href></Icon></IconStyle></Style>
  <Style id="outboundLine"><LineStyle><color>ff2a50b8</color><width>4</width></LineStyle></Style>
  <Style id="returnLine"><LineStyle><color>ffa06c2b</color><width>4</width></LineStyle></Style>
  <Style id="altLine"><LineStyle><color>ff7e7f2a</color><width>3</width></LineStyle></Style>

  <Folder>
    <name>Stops</name>
    
    <Placemark>
      <name>S · Home — Murray, UT</name>
      <styleUrl>#startPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">4748 South Glencrest Road · Starting point</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Home base — starting and ending point of the loop.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 4748 South Glencrest Road, Murray, UT 84107</div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.875831,40.667194,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>1 · Crystal Hot Springs</name>
      <styleUrl>#springPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHRyP.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Developed hot &amp; cold pools · Honeyville, UT</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">About 13 miles north of Brigham City on the way out, Crystal Hot Springs channels natural mineral water into a range of developed pools from cool to scalding, plus a large recreational pool and waterslide — an easy first soak of the trip, right off I-15.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 8215 N Hwy 38, Honeyville, UT 84314</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://www.crystalhotsprings.net/" target="_blank">Illustrative rendering — exact pools not photographed</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.088,41.659,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>2 · Malad: Lottery Ticket Stop</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6N9Fzg.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">27 Bannock St · first town over the Idaho line</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Utah has no state lottery, so whenever Powerball or Mega Millions climbs toward a big jackpot, Utahns make the short drive north across the border to Malad City to buy tickets — gas stations here, especially KJ's Kwik Stop on Main Street, regularly see lines of Utah plates. Malad City is also the seat of Oneida County, settled in the 1860s largely by Welsh immigrants — it's said to have more residents of Welsh descent per capita than anywhere in the U.S. outside Wales. The Oneida Pioneer Museum and 1917 courthouse anchor its small historic downtown a block away.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 27 Bannock St, Malad City, ID 83252</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://www.fox13now.com/news/local-news/northern-utah/utahns-join-in-to-bet-their-luck-for-largest-ever-1-3-billion-powerball" target="_blank">Fox13</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.2462,42.186055,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>3 · Devil Creek Reservoir</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6N9mjn.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">I-15 Exit 22 · scenic overlook</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Tucked into a break in the mountains just north of Malad Pass, Devil Creek Reservoir is stocked with rainbow trout by Idaho Fish &amp; Game. It sits right alongside the highway, with views back down the Malad Valley.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> Devil Creek Reservoir, near I-15 Exit 22, Malad, ID</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://idfg.idaho.gov/ifwis/fishingplanner/water/1122052422984" target="_blank">Idaho Fish &amp; Game</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.204022,42.2994,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>4 · McCammon</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NH9CG.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">US-30 / I-15 Business Loop · "Junction City"</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">McCammon grew up in 1892 as the junction of the Oregon Short Line Railroad and the Utah &amp; Northern Railway, earning it the old nickname "Junction City." It still carries some of that railroad-town character, including the historic Harkness Hotel, and marks the turnoff from I-15 onto US-30 toward Lava Hot Springs.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> McCammon, ID 83250</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McCammon,_Idaho" target="_blank">Wikipedia</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.193022,42.650472,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>5 · Lava Hot Springs</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHJ44.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><img src="https://iili.io/C6NH23l.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><img src="https://iili.io/C6NHFvS.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Main Street · World Famous Hot Pools</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">The mineral hot pools here have drawn visitors for well over a century; the Shoshone-Bannock people are said to have treated the springs as neutral, sacred ground where rival tribes could bathe in peace. Today the state-run Hot Pools sit alongside a separate Olympic-size swimming pool and waterslide complex on the Portneuf River, with Main Street's shops and cafes a short walk away — the turnaround point of the day.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 430 E Main St, Lava Hot Springs, ID 83246</div><div style="margin-top:8px"><b>Nearby dining</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 0;padding-left:18px"><li><b>78 Main Street Eatery</b> &mdash; Classic American comfort food — burgers, sandwiches, hearty breakfasts — with a true small-town vibe.</li><li><b>Portneuf Grille &amp; Lounge</b> &mdash; Northwest-inspired fine dining at Riverside Hot Springs Inn; fresh seafood and aged prime steak.</li><li><b>Riverwalk Thai</b> &mdash; Family-owned Thai food in a converted former gas station near the hot springs.</li><li><b>Wagon Wheel Bar</b> &mdash; A lively Main Street bar with pub fare and a full bar — the classic spot for a drink after soaking in the pools. <a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurant_Review-g35511-d516732-Reviews-Wagon_Wheel_Lounge-Lava_Hot_Springs_Idaho.html" target="_blank">(Tripadvisor)</a></li></ul></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.005689,42.61922,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>6 · Soda Springs Geyser</name>
      <styleUrl>#springPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHKy7.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Geyser Park · erupts hourly</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Soda Springs was settled long before the geyser existed. Native Americans and fur trappers knew its bubbling mineral springs for generations, and it became a well-known 'Oregon Trail Oasis' where 1800s emigrants paused to bathe, drink the naturally carbonated water, and rest. Colonel Patrick Connor established an early army post and settlement, Morristown, near here in 1863; in 1870, Brigham Young formally founded present-day Soda Springs just east of that site as one of his planned Mormon colonies. Decades later, in 1937, drillers looking for a warm-water swimming pool accidentally tapped a pressurized aquifer, creating the geyser so powerful the town had to cap it with a valve — it's now set on a timer to erupt roughly on the hour at Geyser Park downtown.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 39 W 1st S, Soda Springs, ID 83276</div><div style="margin-top:8px"><b>Nearby dining</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 0;padding-left:18px"><li><b>Caribou Jack's Trading Co &amp; BBQ</b> &mdash; Hardware store with an in-house BBQ cafe — Traeger-smoked meats and home-cooked sides.</li><li><b>Arctic Circle Restaurant</b> &mdash; Regional fast-food outpost — a reliable quick stop for burgers and shakes.</li><li><b>Enders Hotel &amp; Restaurant</b> &mdash; Historic hotel dining room downtown, known for classic American comfort food.</li></ul></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.600674,42.653575,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>7 · Hooper Springs Park</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHBje.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Government Dam Rd · naturally carbonated spring</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Hooper Spring was a documented stop on the Oregon and California Trails, where westbound emigrants paused to sample the naturally carbonated "soda" water the town is named for. A small stone pavilion still covers the spring today, and the shaded park around it makes a pleasant short break — about 2 miles north of the geyser, barely off the direct route.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 1805 Government Dam Rd, Soda Springs, ID 83276</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://www.sodaspringsid.com/recreation/hooper_springs_park/index.php" target="_blank">City of Soda Springs</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.59412,42.672109,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>8 · Bear River Massacre Site</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHCZu.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">US-91 near Preston · National Historic Landmark</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">On January 29, 1863, roughly 200 California Volunteer troops under Colonel Patrick Edward Connor attacked a Northwestern Shoshone winter camp led by Chief Bear Hunter, encamped along the Bear River. Estimates of Shoshone deaths range from about 250 up to 400–500 men, women, and children, while the Army reported 21–23 soldiers killed and roughly 50 more wounded — a toll higher than at Sand Creek or Wounded Knee, making it the deadliest single episode of violence between the U.S. Army and Native Americans in the West. A 1932 monument and newer interpretive panels, developed with the Northwestern Band of the Shoshone Nation, mark the bluff overlooking the site, directly on the route into Preston.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> US-91, Preston, ID 83263</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_River_Massacre" target="_blank">Wikipedia</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.877719,42.110151,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>9 · Preston — Historic Downtown</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHxGj.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Franklin County seat · "Napoleon Dynamite"</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Preston's unassuming downtown — including the Franklin County Courthouse, the old Deseret Industries thrift store, and Preston High School — became familiar to millions after Napoleon Dynamite was filmed here on a shoestring budget in 2003. Fans still stop for tater tots and to spot Rex Kwon Do landmarks.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 36 S State St, Preston, ID 83263</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://grahmsguide.com/napoleon-dynamite-filming-locations/" target="_blank">Grahm's Guide</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.876716,42.095438,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>10 · Bear River Hot Springs</name>
      <styleUrl>#springPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHYwF.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Riverside soaking pools · short detour from Preston</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">A low-key, family-owned hot springs resort on the banks of the Bear River, with mineral soaking pools open year-round (hours vary by day). A short detour northeast of downtown Preston, it's a quieter alternative to Lava for a soak on the way home.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 3696 N 1600 E, Preston, ID 83263</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://www.bearriverhotsprings.com/" target="_blank">Illustrative rendering — exact pools not photographed</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.83777,42.16446,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>11 · Maple Grove Hot Springs</name>
      <styleUrl>#springPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHlna.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Riverside pools &amp; glamping · via ID-34 from Preston</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Tucked into a river canyon along ID-34, Maple Grove is a 45-acre soaking retreat with four to six riverside mineral pools, a beach, hiking trails, and glamping yurts and cabins. It's farther off-route than the other springs — worth it only if there's time for the full detour.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 11386 Oneida Narrows Rd, Thatcher, ID 83283</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://www.maplegrovesprings.com/" target="_blank">Illustrative rendering — exact pools not photographed</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.707924,42.30906,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>12 · Logan</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHz6x.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Main Street · Cache Valley</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Cache Valley gets its name from the fur-trapping era of the 1820s: mountain men working for outfits like William Ashley's Rocky Mountain Fur Company wintered in the valley and cached — buried or hid — their beaver pelts and supplies here rather than hauling them out, and the valley hosted a famous trappers' rendezvous in 1826. Early arrivals had called it "Willow Valley" for its riverbank willows, but the cache name stuck. Mormon pioneers settled the valley permanently in the 1850s, founding Logan and, between 1878 and 1891, building the grand limestone Logan Tabernacle that still anchors Main Street today, with the larger Logan Temple visible on the bench above.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 50 N Main St, Logan, UT 84321</div><div style="margin-top:8px"><b>Nearby dining</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 0;padding-left:18px"><li><b>Le Nonne Ristorante Italiano</b> &mdash; Northern Italian cooking and handmade gnocchi, 129 N 100 East. <a href="https://www.cityweekly.net/utah/second-to-nonne-from-northern-italy-to-northern-utah-thereandrsquos-no-better-gnocchi-than-micheliandrsquos/Content?oid=2128297" target="_blank">(City Weekly)</a></li><li><b>Elements Restaurant</b> &mdash; Fine dining at Riverwoods, 35 E 640 South. <a href="https://www.cityweekly.net/utah/elements-fine-dining-in-logan/Content?oid=2153126" target="_blank">(City Weekly)</a></li><li><b>Cafe Sabor</b> &mdash; Tex-Mex in a former train depot, 600 W Center St.</li></ul></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.834766,41.73242,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>13 · Brigham City</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHT3Q.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Main Street, Brigham City</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">The original Box Elder Tabernacle burned in 1896 and was rebuilt within a year; its distinctive candlestick-topped tower and spires make it one of the most photographed buildings in northern Utah, anchoring Brigham City's poplar-lined Main Street. Just south in nearby Perry, Maddox Ranch House Restaurant has served its famous fried chicken and steaks since 1949 — but it's closed Sundays and Mondays, so it won't be an option on this Sunday drive. Brigham City is the last stop before this drive rejoins I-15 south toward Murray.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 251 S Main St, Brigham City, UT 84302</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://maddoxfinefood.com/" target="_blank">Maddox Ranch House</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-112.014901,41.505424,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>14 · Ogden — Union Station</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6NHA8B.jpg" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">Historic 25th Street · railroad, gun &amp; car museums</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Opened in 1924 after fire destroyed its predecessor, Union Station once saw dozens of trains a day pass through Ogden's rail junction connecting the transcontinental line. The restored depot now houses the Utah State Railroad, Browning Firearms, and Browning-Kimball classic-car museums beneath its grand arched windows, with the shops, galleries, and restaurants of historic 25th Street a short walk away — a good last stretch-the-legs stop before the freeway home. Ogden's rail yards also drew large numbers of Greek immigrants in the early 1900s, who lived and worked section and steel gangs on the Oregon Short Line and Union Pacific out of the city — part of the same wave of Greek railroad and mining labor that built Greektowns across Utah.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 2501 Wall Ave, Ogden, UT 84401</div><div style="margin-top:8px"><b>Nearby dining</b><ul style="margin:4px 0 0 0;padding-left:18px"><li><b>Hearth on 25th</b> &mdash; Wood-fired, seasonal New American plates, 195 25th St. <a href="https://www.cityweekly.net/utah/hearth/Content?oid=3027284" target="_blank">(City Weekly)</a></li><li><b>Slackwater Pub &amp; Pizzeria</b> &mdash; Craft beer and wood-fired pizza, 1895 Washington Blvd. <a href="https://www.cityweekly.net/utah/slackwater-pub-and-pizzeria/Content?oid=2158777" target="_blank">(City Weekly)</a></li><li><b>La Crepe OG</b> &mdash; Sweet and savory crepes, 2401 Kiesel Ave. <a href="https://www.cityweekly.net/utah/the-thrill-chill-cult/Content?oid=16969345" target="_blank">(City Weekly)</a></li></ul></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.97972,41.22083,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>15 · Eaglewood Golf Course</name>
      <styleUrl>#midPin</styleUrl>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://iili.io/C6e9ev2.png" style="width:100%;max-width:320px;border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:8px" /><div style="color:#666;font-size:12px;margin-bottom:6px">1110 E Eaglewood Dr · North Salt Lake</div><p style="margin:0 0 10px 0">Where John Saltas and team took 2nd Place Net in the annual Skedros Memorial Greek Open Golf Tournament. And where just last Sunday, Pete made birdie on the 18th hole.</p><div style="margin-bottom:8px"><b>Address:</b> 1110 E Eaglewood Dr, North Salt Lake, UT 84054</div><div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:11px;color:#888">Photo reference: <a href="https://eaglewoodgolf.com/" target="_blank">Illustrative rendering — exact course not photographed</a></div>]]></description>
      <Point><coordinates>-111.888103,40.83194,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>
  <Folder>
    <name>Routes</name>
    
    <Placemark>
